04788 \par
04789     {\cf20 // The properties of popups windows are:}\par
04790     {\cf20 // - They block normal mouse hovering detection outside them. (*)}\par
04791     {\cf20 // - Unless modal, they can be closed by clicking anywhere outside them, or by pressing ESCAPE.}\par
04792     {\cf20 // - Their visibility state (~bool) is held internally by Dear ImGui instead of being held by the programmer as}\par
04793     {\cf20 //   we are used to with regular Begin() calls. User can manipulate the visibility state by calling OpenPopup().}\par
04794     {\cf20 // (*) One can use IsItemHovered(ImGuiHoveredFlags_AllowWhenBlockedByPopup) to bypass it and detect hovering even}\par
04795     {\cf20 //     when normally blocked by a popup.}\par
04796     {\cf20 // Those three properties are connected. The library needs to hold their visibility state BECAUSE it can close}\par
04797     {\cf20 // popups at any time.}\par
04798 \par
04799     {\cf20 // Typical use for regular windows:}\par
04800     {\cf20 //   bool my_tool_is_active = false; if (ImGui::Button("Open")) my_tool_is_active = true; [...] if (my_tool_is_active) Begin("My Tool", &my_tool_is_active) \{ [...] \} End();}\par
04801     {\cf20 // Typical use for popups:}\par
04802     {\cf20 //   if (ImGui::Button("Open")) ImGui::OpenPopup("MyPopup"); if (ImGui::BeginPopup("MyPopup") \{ [...] EndPopup(); \}}\par
04803 \par
04804     {\cf20 // With popups we have to go through a library call (here OpenPopup) to manipulate the visibility state.}\par
04805     {\cf20 // This may be a bit confusing at first but it should quickly make sense. Follow on the examples below.}\par
